WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.178 --> 00:00:01.778
Hello.

2
00:00:01.678 --> 00:00:02.972
I'm Darlene Kuehl.

3
00:00:02.972 --> 00:00:07.605
I'm a CNA, a certified nursing assistant, at
Kestrel Falls Community Hospital.

4
00:00:07.605 --> 00:00:11.371
I told my manager I wanted to be included
in planning for the new system,

5
00:00:11.371 --> 00:00:14.052
and Laura Hemmingsen asked me to
share what I see.

6
00:00:14.052 --> 00:00:18.288
I've been a CNA here nineteen years,
all on Med-Surg, day shift.

7
00:00:18.288 --> 00:00:22.631
I take vitals, I help patients eat and
walk, I help them to the bathroom,

8
00:00:22.631 --> 00:00:25.569
and I chart intake and
output, the I and O.

9
00:00:25.569 --> 00:00:29.098
I'm usually the first one to notice
when a patient isn't right.

10
00:00:29.098 --> 00:00:31.431
A little confused, not eating,
breathing a little faster.

11
00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:33.078
Can I tell you a secret about
charting in Plexmoor?

12
00:00:33.078 --> 00:00:38.538
Half the floor writes vitals on a paper sheet
first and types them into Plexmoor

13
00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.471
later, because the flowsheet is so slow.

14
00:00:40.471 --> 00:00:41.245
That's not safe.

15
00:00:41.245 --> 00:00:44.019
A number can get copied wrong, or
entered an hour late.

16
00:00:44.019 --> 00:00:48.807
If a blood pressure is low and it sits on
a paper sheet in my pocket,

17
00:00:48.807 --> 00:00:50.692
the nurse doesn't see it on the screen.

18
00:00:50.692 --> 00:00:52.954
I tell her out loud, of course, but
the system doesn't know.

19
00:00:52.954 --> 00:00:54.967
Why is it so slow?

20
00:00:54.967 --> 00:01:00.965
There's only a couple of computers on wheels
for the whole hall, and they're old.

21
00:01:00.965 --> 00:01:03.607
Then you have to log in, find the
patient, open the flowsheet,

22
00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:07.348
scroll to the right time, and
click into each box.

23
00:01:07.348 --> 00:01:09.167
By then I've got three call lights going.

24
00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:11.897
So I write it down and catch
up when I can.

25
00:01:11.897 --> 00:01:12.497
Everybody does it.

26
00:01:11.897 --> 00:01:14.601
Nobody likes it.

27
00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:17.321
So nobody has to convince me the
old system has problems.

28
00:01:17.321 --> 00:01:20.863
What worries me is that people like me
get left out of the plan.

29
00:01:20.863 --> 00:01:24.328
The meetings are for doctors and managers.

30
00:01:24.328 --> 00:01:26.699
Then the new system shows up and the
CNAs find out on the day.

31
00:01:26.699 --> 00:01:30.385
That's what happened last time.

32
00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:32.024
We got a one-page sheet and a login.

33
00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:34.249
What would it take for me to
support the change?

34
00:01:34.249 --> 00:01:36.422
Show me, clearly, why we have to change.

35
00:01:36.422 --> 00:01:41.375
Not "because the vendor said so." Tell
me what happens if we don't.

36
00:01:41.375 --> 00:01:44.402
Then show me that the new one
fixes the vitals problem.

37
00:01:44.402 --> 00:01:46.358
Let me try entering a set of vitals on it.

38
00:01:46.358 --> 00:01:51.124
If it's faster and I can do it at the bedside,
I'll tell everybody on this floor.

39
00:01:51.124 --> 00:01:52.780
They listen to me.

40
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:53.816
Who listens to me?

41
00:01:53.816 --> 00:01:55.145
The other CNAs, for sure.

42
00:01:55.145 --> 00:01:57.055
A lot of the new ones I trained.

43
00:01:57.055 --> 00:02:00.251
And plenty of the nurses.

44
00:02:00.251 --> 00:02:03.146
When something new comes out, they
ask me if it's any good.

45
00:02:03.146 --> 00:02:06.485
If I say it's fine, they try it.

46
00:02:06.485 --> 00:02:07.203
If I roll my eyes, well.

47
00:02:07.203 --> 00:02:12.743
What I don't want is a pamphlet that says
"exciting changes are coming." We've

48
00:02:12.743 --> 00:02:13.343
heard that before.

49
00:02:12.743 --> 00:02:14.260
Talk to us like adults.

50
00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.852
Tell us what will be hard.

51
00:02:15.852 --> 00:02:20.731
Tell us it'll be slower the first couple
of weeks, if that's true.

52
00:02:20.731 --> 00:02:21.331
We can handle hard.

53
00:02:20.731 --> 00:02:24.511
What we can't handle is being surprised.

54
00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:26.927
For CNA training, make it
hands-on and make it short.

55
00:02:26.927 --> 00:02:32.633
Thirty minutes on the stuff we actually do:
vitals, I and O, turning schedules,

56
00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:33.908
meal percentages.

57
00:02:33.908 --> 00:02:37.197
And do it on the unit, on the same
kind of device we'll use,

58
00:02:37.197 --> 00:02:39.375
not in a classroom on a desktop.

59
00:02:39.375 --> 00:02:42.327
And please, include the night CNAs.

60
00:02:42.327 --> 00:02:44.129
They always get forgotten.

61
00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:48.463
If you asked me to help test the vitals
screens before go-live, I'd be glad to.

62
00:02:48.463 --> 00:02:52.292
Just give me the time off the floor to do it,
so my patients aren't short a CNA.

63
00:02:52.292 --> 00:02:55.901
And here's how I'll know, after
go-live, that the change worked.

64
00:02:55.901 --> 00:03:00.421
When nobody on my floor carries a
paper vitals sheet anymore.

65
00:03:00.421 --> 00:03:01.020
That's my test.

66
00:03:00.421 --> 00:03:05.289
If we're still writing on paper six
months later, it didn't work,

67
00:03:05.289 --> 00:03:06.815
no matter what the report says.

68
00:03:06.815 --> 00:03:07.415
And ask the CNAs.

69
00:03:06.815 --> 00:03:09.800
Don't just ask the managers
how it's going.

